\begin_layout Title
The Effect of Tuition Decreases for STEM Majors on Job Placement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
September 5, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to the US News, 80% of the fastest growing occupations in the
 United States depend on the mastery of mathematics and scientific knowledge
 and skills.
 However, there has been a signficant shortage of qualified graduates with
 the necessary skills to occupy these positions.
 To address the issue, Florida Governor Rick Scott recently suggested that
 students majoring in science, technology, engineering, and math (STEM)
 programs be offered lower tuition than those studying in the liberal arts
 or social sciences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It has been suggested that college costs can play a factor when students
 choose a major.
 For example, the University of Texas at Austin began charging engineering
 students higher tuition when costs of mainting the programs increased.
 Soon after, the engineering program experienced a drop in enrollment.
 Also, it has been shown that for every $1,000 tuition increases, 5% fewer
 students will engage in the program.
\begin_inset Foot
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Differential Pricing in Undergraduate Education: Effects on Degree Production
 by Field.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Kevin Strange, 2011.
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With his proposal, Governor Scott hopes to address the critical issue of
 worker shortage in high demand sectors by encouraging more students to
 enroll in STEM programs.
 However, those in the liberal arts and social sciences have began protesting
 the possible measure claiming that tuition disparity is unjust.
 In fact, some of the college majors with the lowest unemployment rates
 come from non-STEM programs.
 While 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

high skill, high demand
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 is an ambiguous statement, no one seems to be discussing the proposal's
 intended effect.
 Would a decrease in tuition for students enrolled in STEM programs actually
 cause an increase in job placement in fields associated with those respective
 majors? 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The goal of my research project would be to answer the important question
 just posed.
 All universities keep a detailed record of students' majors and graduation
 years; many of them even follow through with surveys regarding current
 working status while soliciting donations from alumni.
 The observations will represent individuals, while the dependent variable
 will be a discrete variable that takes the value 1 if an individual is
 employed in a job that corresponds to his major, and 0 otherwise.
 The independent variable will be a continuous variable corresponding to
 the tuition rate paid by the individual.
 Thus, I will measure the effect of tuition decreases on job placement by
 using a binary response model and controlling for family background, university
 attended, chosen major, program rankings, high school rankings, and SAT
 scores.
\end_layout

